WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.267
Encoder-only models are designed to build strong contextual representations of an input sequence.

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:12.922
Every token can usually attend to tokens on both sides, subject to the model’s masking rules.

00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:19.367
That bidirectional context is useful when the goal is to understand or label existing text rather than

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:23.412
generate a long continuation token by token.

00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:29.112
BERT and RoBERTa are classic examples of encoder-style architectures.

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:34.417
Common applications include document classification, sentiment analysis, named entity recognition,

00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:40.212
token classification, question-answering variants, and feature extraction.

00:00:40.212 --> 00:00:46.692
Sentence-embedding systems are often built by adapting encoder models so a whole sentence or passage maps to a

00:00:46.692 --> 00:00:50.617
useful vector.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:55.207
When you see a task whose output is a label, a set of token labels, or a representation,

00:00:55.207 --> 00:00:59.982
an encoder model is often a good place to start.

00:00:59.982 --> 00:01:04.900
The model architecture still matters, but the task should guide the choice.
